window.PYNAVIS_SEARCH = [
{t:"Settings",p:"pyNavis",u:"settings.html",b:"One window over the parts of pyNavis you are meant to change: theme, ribbon hints, where extensions load from, and the engine paths."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Settings",u:"settings.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # pyNavis keeps its configuration in one JSON file, %APPDATA%\\pyNavis\\config.json . Most of that file is maintained by pyNavis itself. Settings is the window over the handful of keys a person is meant to change, so nobody has to open the file in a text editor to switch the theme or add an extension folder. The Settings window: Appearance, Shortcuts, extension folders"},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Settings",u:"settings.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Click Settings . The window opens with the current values. Appearance. Pick a theme, or leave it following Navisworks. Two checkboxes control the hints on button captions: a dot after a tool that has a keyboard shortcut, on by default, and an arrow after a tool that has a Shift+Click action, off by default. The box beside each checkbox is the glyph it draws. Leav"},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Settings",u:"settings.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Which tool holds which shortcut is not edited here. That is the Shortcuts editor's job. Engine paths take effect only after Navisworks restarts. A Reload cannot swap a Python engine under a running ribbon. The window edits only the keys listed above. Shortcut overrides, dock panel slot assignments and window layouts stay in the file untouched, because the tools that "},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Settings",u:"settings.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Point pyNavis at a shared network folder of extensions so a whole team gets the same tools, then keep your own experiments in a second, local folder. Turn the Shift+Click arrow on while learning the tab, then off again once you know which tools carry settings."},
{t:"Good to know",p:"Settings",u:"settings.html#good-to-know",b:"id=\"good-to-know\">Good to know # Cancel says nothing and changes nothing. Every key the window writes is described in the authoring guide's config.json reference , which is where to look if you would rather edit the file directly."},
{t:"Shortcuts",p:"pyNavis",u:"shortcuts.html",b:"The keyboard shortcut editor: every tool that can carry a chord, the chord it has now, and a field to press a new one into."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Shortcuts",u:"shortcuts.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Every pyNavis tool can have a keyboard shortcut, and the shipped tools come with a few already bound. This editor lists all of them, searchable, and lets you change, clear or restore any of them. Your changes are stored in config.json and the tools' own defaults are never touched, so a Reset always has something to go back to. The Shortcuts editor with a chord bein"},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Shortcuts",u:"shortcuts.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Click Shortcuts . Type in the search box to narrow the list. Click the field on a row and press the keys you want, for example Ctrl + Shift + F . The field shows the chord and the row's badge reads \"custom\". Backspace in a field clears the binding so the tool has none. Esc leaves the field as it was. A chord already used by another tool is flagged on the row, nam"},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Shortcuts",u:"shortcuts.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # A chord always runs the tool's primary action, never its Shift+Click settings. Chords fire when the 3D view or the ribbon has focus. A dialog with a text box takes the keys for itself, as it should. Unless allowed in Settings, a chord must include Ctrl or Alt. Navisworks and some graphics drivers claim a few chords for themselves; if a chord never fires, rebind it. T"},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Shortcuts",u:"shortcuts.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Switch off a chord you keep hitting by accident rather than living with it. The six Section Nudge chords live here too, under their Nudge titles, which is how you move them off Ctrl + Alt on a machine where the display driver owns those keys."},
{t:"Good to know",p:"Shortcuts",u:"shortcuts.html#good-to-know",b:"id=\"good-to-know\">Good to know # Bindings are keyed by the tool's folder path, so a tool that moves to another panel loses its override and goes back to its default. The shipped tools do not move between releases."},
{t:"Console",p:"pyNavis",u:"console.html",b:"An interactive Python console running inside Navisworks, with the same API and helpers your scripts get."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Console",u:"console.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Opens a window where you type Python and run it against the open model, in the same process and with the same pynavis library the ribbon tools use. It is the fastest way to answer \"what does this API call return on this model\" before writing it into a tool, and a handy calculator for one-off jobs that do not deserve a button."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Console",u:"console.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Click Console . It opens with no document and with one alike. Type in the lower pane. Run with Ctrl + Enter or the Run button. Output collects in the upper pane. Up and Down at the edges of the input walk back through what you have already run. Start with from pynavis import selection, toast and selection.get_items() : the list you get back is the same one every "},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Console",u:"console.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # It is a console, not an editor: there is no file, no save, and history lives for the session only. Anything worth keeping belongs in a bundle. Code runs on the Navisworks UI thread, like every tool. A long loop freezes the window until it finishes. The IronPython engine runs here. A bundle that needs numpy runs on CPython and cannot be tried in this console."},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Console",u:"console.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Count something the interface will not: items in the selection by category, saved viewpoints per folder, clash results per status. Try a search condition and see what it matches before building a selection set."},
{t:"Good to know",p:"Console",u:"console.html#good-to-know",b:"id=\"good-to-know\">Good to know # The Python API reference lists every helper available here. If you find yourself pasting the same lines twice, the quickstart shows how to turn them into a button in five minutes."},
{t:"Reload",p:"pyNavis",u:"reload.html",b:"Rescan every extension folder and rebuild the ribbon, without restarting Navisworks."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Reload",u:"reload.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Reads every extension folder again and rebuilds the pyNavis tab from what it finds. New bundles appear, renamed ones move, changed captions, tooltips, icons and shortcuts take effect, and each extension's startup.py runs again. Editing a tool and clicking Reload is the whole development loop."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Reload",u:"reload.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Change something on disk: a new folder, an edited bundle.yaml , a new icon. Click Reload . A toast says when the tab is rebuilt. Click your button. A script edit alone does not even need this: script.py is read fresh on every click."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Reload",u:"reload.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Three things need a Navisworks restart instead: a changed engine path, a *.lib folder added or removed, and new dock panel slots. Reload rebuilds pyNavis' tab only. Other add-ins are untouched. Open dock panels are repainted with fresh controls; a panel that was mid-edit loses the edit."},
{t:"Good to know",p:"Reload",u:"reload.html#good-to-know",b:"id=\"good-to-know\">Good to know # Reload writes ==== pyNavis reload ==== to %APPDATA%\\pyNavis\\logs\\pyNavis.log and then lists every bundle it skipped and why, so a button that fails to appear is one log line away from an explanation."},
{t:"Panel slots",p:"pyNavis",u:"panel-slots.html",b:"Make more dock panel slots when the five that ship are all claimed."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Panel slots",u:"panel-slots.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # A dock panel bundle needs a slot in the loader to live in, and five ship with pyNavis. Each dock panel claims one for life. When a sixth panel arrives this tool generates a small satellite assembly holding more slots, up to 95 extra."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Panel slots",u:"panel-slots.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Open the pyNavis panel's flyout (the small arrow on the panel title) and click Panel slots . It reports how many slots exist and asks how many extra you want. Restart Navisworks. The new slots exist only after that."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Panel slots",u:"panel-slots.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Every slot is visible in View > Windows as \"pyNavis Panel N\", claimed or not. Asking for 95 puts 95 entries in that menu. The satellite is written beside the loader in %APPDATA%\\Autodesk\\ApplicationPlugins\\pyNavis.bundle , so it is per user."},
{t:"Good to know",p:"Panel slots",u:"panel-slots.html#good-to-know",b:"id=\"good-to-know\">Good to know # You will not need this unless you install extensions that bring their own dock panels. The two shipped panels use two of the five slots."},
{t:"Remember",p:"Selection",u:"remember.html",b:"Store the current selection in the document's memory, replacing whatever was there."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Remember",u:"remember.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Replaces the memory with the current selection and toasts how many items went in. This is the starting point for everything else on the panel."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Remember",u:"remember.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Select anything: a click in the view, a Find Items result, a selection set. Press Ctrl + Shift + M or click Remember . Carry on. Hide things, section, click elsewhere. The memory keeps what you had."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Remember",u:"remember.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # One memory per document. Remember replaces it; use Add to grow it instead. Items are stored by their path in the model tree. If the model is republished with a different structure, some items may not resolve on Recall, and the toast will say how many."},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Remember",u:"remember.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Before a risky operation, Remember the selection so a mis-click costs nothing. Remember at the end of the day; the memory is still there tomorrow."},
{t:"Recall",p:"Selection",u:"recall.html",b:"Select whatever is in memory, replacing the current selection."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Recall",u:"recall.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Resolves every stored item back to the model and selects it. Items that are no longer there, because the model was reloaded or the item deleted, are counted in the toast and skipped rather than failing the whole recall."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Recall",u:"recall.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Press Ctrl + Shift + R or click Recall . The selection is back. Zoom to it with Navisworks' own Zoom Selected if you moved."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Recall",u:"recall.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Recall replaces the current selection. To combine, Recall first and then add to the selection by hand, or use Add and Intersect in the other direction. An empty memory toasts \"Memory is empty\" and leaves the selection alone."},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Recall",u:"recall.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Hand a colleague a selection: Save as Set puts it in the document; Recall is the private version that stays yours."},
{t:"Add",p:"Selection",u:"add.html",b:"Add the current selection to memory, keeping what was already there."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Add",u:"add.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Unions the current selection into the memory. Use it to build up a set over several passes through a model: find some pipes on level 2, Add, find the rest on level 3, Add."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Add",u:"add.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Remember a first selection. Select more. Click Add . The toast gives the new total. Repeat, then Recall to see the whole set."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Add",u:"add.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # An item already in memory is not counted twice."},
{t:"Subtract",p:"Selection",u:"subtract.html",b:"Remove the current selection from memory."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Subtract",u:"subtract.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Takes the current selection out of the memory. The way to carve the unwanted part out of a stored set: Recall, click the items that should not be there, Subtract."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Subtract",u:"subtract.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Recall the memory. Select only the items to drop. Click Subtract . Recall again to check."},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Subtract",u:"subtract.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Everything except: Remember a whole system, select the part that is finished, Subtract, and the memory is the outstanding work."},
{t:"Intersect",p:"Selection",u:"intersect.html",b:"Keep only the items that are both in memory and selected right now."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Intersect",u:"intersect.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Two searches reduced to what they agree on: one stored in memory, one live in the selection. What survives is the overlap."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Intersect",u:"intersect.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Run a search, say everything on Level 3, and Remember it. Run another, say every item with Fire Rating set, and select it. Click Intersect . The memory is now the fire-rated items on Level 3. Recall to select them."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Intersect",u:"intersect.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Intersect with an empty selection empties the memory, because nothing is in both. The toast says so."},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Intersect",u:"intersect.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Any two Find Items searches that Navisworks cannot combine in one query."},
{t:"Previous and Next",p:"Selection",u:"previous-next.html",b:"Select and zoom to one remembered item at a time, walking the list from the keyboard."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Previous and Next",u:"previous-next.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Selects a single item from the memory and zooms to it, then the one after it, then the next. This is how you walk a list of items you found earlier without losing your place: review each clash element, each unresolved comment, each item on a snag list."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Previous and Next",u:"previous-next.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Remember the list of items to review. Press Ctrl + Shift + Right . The first item is selected and the camera zooms to it. Keep pressing. Both directions wrap around, so Next at the end returns to the start."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Previous and Next",u:"previous-next.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # The camera move cannot be undone. Save a viewpoint first if you want to come back. The order is the order the items were stored in, which is the order Navisworks listed them at Remember time."},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Previous and Next",u:"previous-next.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # A quick model walkthrough for a meeting: Remember the ten things to show, then step through them with one hand while talking."},
{t:"Forget",p:"Selection",u:"forget.html",b:"Empty the memory for this document."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Forget",u:"forget.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Empties the memory for this document and reports how many items were dropped. Other documents keep theirs."},
{t:"Good to know",p:"Forget",u:"forget.html#good-to-know",b:"id=\"good-to-know\">Good to know # You rarely need this: Remember replaces the memory anyway. It is there for the moment you want to be certain nothing is stored, and for tidying up after a job. To delete the stored files for every document at once, use Purge in the More menu."},
{t:"More menu",p:"Selection",u:"more-menu.html",b:"Show what is in memory, promote it to a selection set, or delete the stored memory files."},
{t:"What it does",p:"More menu",u:"more-menu.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # The pulldown at the end of the panel holds the three things you do to the memory rather than with it."},
{t:"Show Contents",p:"More menu",u:"more-menu.html#show-contents",b:"id=\"show-contents\">Show Contents # Lists the memory in the output window, one row per item with the model it came from and a link that selects it, under a line naming the document it was remembered from and when. Long memories are cut off at 500 rows. An empty memory prints a line telling you to select something and press Remember."},
{t:"Save as Set",p:"More menu",u:"more-menu.html#save-as-set",b:"id=\"save-as-set\">Save as Set # Promotes the memory to a native Navisworks selection set. It asks for a name, offering \"Memory\" plus the date and time, and creates the set. Unlike the memory, a selection set is stored inside the document, so this marks the document modified and the set travels with the file. This is how a private memory becomes something the team can see."},
{t:"Purge",p:"More menu",u:"more-menu.html#purge",b:"id=\"purge\">Purge # Deletes the stored memory files for every document, not just this one. It counts the files first and asks for confirmation naming that number, and says so quietly when there is nothing to delete."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"More menu",u:"more-menu.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Save as Set makes a static set of the items as they are now, not a search, so it does not follow the model when new items match."},
{t:"Clash Report",p:"Clash",u:"clash-report.html",b:"A one-screen count of every clash test by status, with a CSV export for the report."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Clash Report",u:"clash-report.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Prints one row per Clash Detective test with its total and the count in each status: New, Active, Reviewed, Approved and Resolved. A line underneath totals the tests and the results. It is the number you read out at the start of the meeting, and the evidence line in the weekly report, without opening Clash Detective and adding the columns up by eye."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Clash Report",u:"clash-report.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Run or refresh your clash tests in Clash Detective as usual. Click Clash Report . The table appears in the output window. Answer the question that follows: export the same table to CSV? Yes asks where to save it."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Clash Report",u:"clash-report.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Counts only. It does not list individual clashes; that is what the Clash Detective report and Clash Grouper are for. Needs Navisworks Manage. Simulate has no Clash Detective, and the tool says so in a dialog rather than failing. Groups count as one result each, the same way Clash Detective's own summary counts them."},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Clash Report",u:"clash-report.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Export the CSV every week into the same folder and you have a trend line of open clashes for free. Paste the table into the meeting minutes as the state of play at the time of the meeting."},
{t:"Clash Grouper",p:"Clash",u:"clash-grouper.html",b:"Turn a test's results into reviewable issues, grouping by root cause, element, level, grid or proximity, with a live preview."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Clash Grouper",u:"clash-grouper.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Three hundred clashes between the same two runs of pipework are one problem. This tool says so before anyone opens a report. It groups a test's results into issues, either by the element that causes them or by a chain of rules you choose, shows you the groups it would make, and only then writes them into Clash Detective."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Clash Grouper",u:"clash-grouper.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Click Clash Grouper . The dialog lists the tests in the document and fills in their result counts as it counts them. Tick the tests to group. Leave smart grouping on to group by the root-cause element, or switch it off and chain your own rules: element on side A or side B, proximity cluster, nearest level, grid intersection, source model, status, or assigned to. "},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Clash Grouper",u:"clash-grouper.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Ungroup removes only the groups this tool made. Groups made by hand are never touched, which also means the tool cannot tidy up someone else's grouping. Grid and level rules need grids and levels in the model. A model exported without them falls back to proximity. The proximity cluster distance starts at two metres and is the setting most worth tuning: too small and "},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Clash Grouper",u:"clash-grouper.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Group by assigned to before a meeting so each trade sees only its own pile. Group by source model to find out which link is generating most of the noise this week."},
{t:"Good to know",p:"Clash Grouper",u:"clash-grouper.html#good-to-know",b:"id=\"good-to-know\">Good to know # Shift+Click stores presets in smart_clash_grouper.json ; every run can still change all of them in the dialog. The groups it writes are ordinary Clash Detective groups and behave like any other in the report."},
{t:"Clear Clash",p:"Clash",u:"clear-clash.html",b:"Point at two faces and slide the selected object until it clears, then read off exactly how far it moved."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Clear Clash",u:"clear-clash.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Objects clash, and the usual next step is to drag one with Item Tools until it clears, read the offset and type it into the authoring tool. This does the dragging. Select the object that should move, point at the face of it that is in the way and the face it must clear, and it slides exactly far enough along those faces' normal, then tells you how far, ready to typ"},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Clear Clash",u:"clear-clash.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Select the object that should move, say the pipe. Click Clear Clash . The native Point to Point measure opens and the banner along the bottom of the window asks for the face of the pipe that must move. Click the underside of the pipe. The banner now asks for the face it must clear. Click the top of the beam. The pipe slides up until its underside is past the beam"},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Clear Clash",u:"clear-clash.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Only the two faces are considered. A part of the object that sticks out past the face you clicked is not checked against the obstacle. The two faces must be parallel. A vertical face against a beam top is refused on the banner, and nothing moves. The move is a Navisworks transform, the same one Item Tools makes. It changes the review model only, never the source file"},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Clear Clash",u:"clear-clash.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # A quick \"what if\": move the pipe, see whether it now hits the ceiling, undo. Sloping soffits and rotated walls work as well as floors, because the faces come from the objects' own triangles rather than the world axes."},
{t:"Good to know",p:"Clear Clash",u:"clear-clash.html#good-to-know",b:"id=\"good-to-know\">Good to know # Esc, a right-click or choosing another tool cancels quietly. The clearance is stored with its unit in resolve_clash.json , so 25 mm stays 25 mm in a feet-and-inches document."},
{t:"Set Gap",p:"Clash",u:"set-gap.html",b:"Point at two faces and type the gap you want between them; the selected object slides the exact difference, tighter or apart, clash or no clash."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Set Gap",u:"set-gap.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Clear Clash only moves an object that is in the way, and only away. Coordination is mostly the other cases: a run of services that should sit tighter to free a corridor, or a tray that needs another 100 mm of air for a duct to pass. Select the object that should move, point at a face on it and the face it should sit against, type the gap you want, and it slides alo"},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Set Gap",u:"set-gap.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Select the object that should move, say the cable tray. Click Set Gap . The native Point to Point measure opens and the banner along the bottom of the window asks for the face of the tray that must move. Click the top of the tray. The banner now asks for the face it should sit against. Click the underside of the duct. A prompt reads \"Tray 42 is 350 mm from Duct 7"},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Set Gap",u:"set-gap.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Only the two faces are considered. Pulling an object tighter can push another part of it into something else; the tool does not check. The two faces must be parallel. Skewed faces are refused on the banner before the prompt opens, so you never type a number that cannot be used. The gap cannot be negative. To bury one object in another on purpose, use Item Tools. The "},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Set Gap",u:"set-gap.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Clearing a clash with a set gap in one go: it also moves objects that are inside each other, and says how deep the clash was. Checking whether a tighter arrangement fits: set the gap, look, undo."},
{t:"Good to know",p:"Set Gap",u:"set-gap.html#good-to-know",b:"id=\"good-to-know\">Good to know # Esc, a right-click or cancelling the prompt leaves everything as it was, quietly. The default gap is stored with its unit in set_gap.json ; the number you type in the prompt is used once and does not change the default."},
{t:"True Distance",p:"Clash",u:"true-distance.html",b:"The real perpendicular distance between two measured faces, unaffected by project rotation or an off-square click."},
{t:"What it does",p:"True Distance",u:"true-distance.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # A rotated project makes the measure tool's X, Y and Z readouts meaningless, and the straight line overshoots whenever the two clicks are not exactly opposite each other. This reports the gap, not the line: the measured vector projected onto the faces' shared normal, which is the number a tape held square to the faces would give."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"True Distance",u:"true-distance.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Click True Distance . The native Point to Point tool opens. Click a point on the first face, then a point on the second. Snap to corners if you like; the result is the same. Read the banner across the bottom of the window. Green: both faces found and parallel. Amber: only one face found, measured square to that one. Red: no face under either point."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"True Distance",u:"true-distance.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Both points must sit on real geometry. A point in empty space has no face. Faces more than the tolerance apart in angle are reported as not parallel, with the angle, and the reading falls back to the first face. The banner stays about eight seconds; the dimension drawn in the view stays until the next run."},
{t:"Other uses",p:"True Distance",u:"true-distance.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Floor to ceiling heights: horizontal faces are parallel faces too. Checking a clearance someone claims was fixed, without trusting a diagonal line."},
{t:"Good to know",p:"True Distance",u:"true-distance.html#good-to-know",b:"id=\"good-to-know\">Good to know # On an imperial document the reading matches the native readout style, and the rounding fraction is yours to set. Settings live in true_distance.json ."},
{t:"Get Coordinates",p:"Data",u:"get-coordinates.html",b:"Click a point in the view and its coordinates land on the clipboard, snapped the way a measurement snaps."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Get Coordinates",u:"get-coordinates.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Puts the view into a pick that runs on the native Measure tool, so the pointer snaps to vertices, edges and line ends exactly as a measurement does. The point that reaches the clipboard is the snapped one, written as X, Y, Z at three decimals in model units, which is the shape Go to Coordinates reads back."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Get Coordinates",u:"get-coordinates.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Click Get Coordinates . Click the point. The cursor snaps as you hover. Paste wherever the point is needed: a report, a survey schedule, an email, or Go to Coordinates."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Get Coordinates",u:"get-coordinates.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Model units, not a shared coordinate system. If the model is offset from the survey grid, so is the point. Esc, a right-click or picking another tool cancels and copies nothing."},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Get Coordinates",u:"get-coordinates.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Report exactly where a clash is to someone who does not have the model open."},
{t:"Go to Coordinates",p:"Data",u:"go-to-coordinates.html",b:"Move the view to a set of coordinates and mark the point with a cross."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Go to Coordinates",u:"go-to-coordinates.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # The other half of the pair. A point copied by Get Coordinates, or pasted out of a clash report or a survey schedule, is one click from being on screen. The camera keeps the direction it is already looking and only moves, standing five metres back from the point, so the angle you set up survives and the point lands in the middle of the view. A cross labelled with th"},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Go to Coordinates",u:"go-to-coordinates.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Copy a point from anywhere: three numbers separated by commas, spaces, semicolons or tabs, with or without brackets or X=/Y=/Z= labels. Click Go to Coordinates . The box opens already filled if the clipboard held three numbers. Click OK. The camera moves and the cross appears. Orbit straight away and the view turns around the point, because the focal distance mov"},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Go to Coordinates",u:"go-to-coordinates.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Three numbers or nothing. A comma always separates, so \"1,5 2 3\" is four numbers and is refused rather than read as a European decimal. One marker at a time. A new point replaces the cross; Shift+Click removes it."},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Go to Coordinates",u:"go-to-coordinates.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Walk a list of survey points from a spreadsheet: copy, click, look, next row."},
{t:"Select by IDs",p:"Data",u:"select-by-ids.html",b:"Paste a column of Revit element IDs and the elements are selected in the federated model, without opening Revit."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Select by IDs",u:"select-by-ids.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Selects elements by Revit element ID, in a federated model. Paste a column of IDs out of a clash report, a schedule or an email and the elements are selected in the view, zoomed to or isolated according to the settings. It looks in both places an element ID survives federation: the \"Element ID\" property tab an NWC exported from Revit carries, and the \"[123456]\" suf"},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Select by IDs",u:"select-by-ids.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Copy the IDs. One per line, or separated by commas or spaces; other text between them is ignored. Click Select by IDs . The box shows the IDs it found for approval and editing. Click OK. Above one ID a progress window names the ID being looked for and offers Cancel. What is found replaces the current selection."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Select by IDs",u:"select-by-ids.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Only real elements match. Nested parts under a family instance carry an \"Element ID\" tab holding the type's ID, the same number on every instance, so the tools only accept nodes the exporter marked as elements. A pasted type ID finds nothing rather than selecting the first instance of that family. One ID resolves to one item by default and stops at the first hit, aro"},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Select by IDs",u:"select-by-ids.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Round-trip with Revit: IDs of Selection in Navisworks, Select by ID in Revit, or the reverse. Check a list of \"fixed\" elements from an email against the current model."},
{t:"IDs of Selection",p:"Data",u:"ids-of-selection.html",b:"Copy the Revit element IDs of the selected items to the clipboard."},
{t:"What it does",p:"IDs of Selection",u:"ids-of-selection.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # The reverse trip. Resolves an ID for each selected item, reading it off the nearest ancestor that is a real element, because a click in the view usually lands on a geometry leaf below the element. The list goes to the clipboard, ready for Select by IDs, for Revit's own Select by ID, or for a report."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"IDs of Selection",u:"ids-of-selection.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Select the items, in the view or from a set. Click IDs of Selection . The toast names a single ID or counts several, and says how many selected items carry no ID at all. Paste."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"IDs of Selection",u:"ids-of-selection.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Items with no element ID, such as a whole file node or non-Revit geometry, are counted in the toast and skipped."},
{t:"Other uses",p:"IDs of Selection",u:"ids-of-selection.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Tell a modeller exactly which elements to fix, in the one identifier both tools share."},
{t:"Excel Sets",p:"Data",u:"excel-sets.html",b:"Export every selection set to a workbook, one row per condition, or import a workbook back as new sets."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Excel Sets",u:"excel-sets.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Round-trips selection sets through an Excel workbook. Export to review a model's sets in a spreadsheet or hand them to someone, edit the rows, and import them back as new sets, or carry a library of standard sets from one federated model to the next."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Excel Sets",u:"excel-sets.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Click Excel Sets and choose Export or Import. Export asks where to save an .xlsx and writes one row per search condition. A set with several conditions repeats its folder and name once per condition. A set with no conditions, including a static set, still gets a row with the condition columns blank so it stays visible. Import asks for a workbook, checks every row"},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Excel Sets",u:"excel-sets.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Search sets only travel as their conditions. A static set exports as a name with no conditions and is skipped on import, because its members belong to the original file. Import creates new sets; it does not update sets that already exist with the same name. Property names must match what the target model calls them. A set that searches for \"Level\" in one exporter's v"},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Excel Sets",u:"excel-sets.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # An office standard: keep one workbook of the sets every project should have and import it into each new federated model. Review a model's sets without opening the model: the workbook reads as a list."},
{t:"Export Viewpoints",p:"Data",u:"export-viewpoints.html",b:"Every saved viewpoint to a CSV: name, folder, type and camera position."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Export Viewpoints",u:"export-viewpoints.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Writes every saved viewpoint in the document to a CSV file with the columns Name, Folder, Type, CameraX, CameraY and CameraZ. For an audit of what a model's saved views actually are, or to diff two issues of the same model."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Export Viewpoints",u:"export-viewpoints.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Click Export Viewpoints . Choose where to save. The name offered is the document's title plus _viewpoints.csv ."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Export Viewpoints",u:"export-viewpoints.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Animations and cuts have no camera of their own, so those rows are written with the three position columns blank rather than dropped. Camera position only. Direction, section planes and hidden items are not exported; use Copy State to carry those."},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Export Viewpoints",u:"export-viewpoints.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Compare two exports in a spreadsheet to see which views were added, renamed or moved between issues."},
{t:"Element ID Settings",p:"Data",u:"element-id-settings.html",b:"How Select by IDs and IDs of Selection find and copy element IDs. One store, shared by both."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Element ID Settings",u:"element-id-settings.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # The settings both element ID tools share, in the panel flyout so they can be found without knowing about Shift+Click. There is one store behind them, because which property holds the ID is a fact about the model rather than about the button that reads it. The Element ID Settings window."},
{t:"The settings",p:"Element ID Settings",u:"element-id-settings.html#the-settings",b:"id=\"the-settings\">The settings # Property category and property , which start at \"Element ID\" and \"Value\", the tab and field the Revit exporter writes. A blank box falls back to the default rather than saving an empty value that would make every search fail silently. Zoom onto what was selected, on by default. Isolate , which hides everything else as well, off by default. Fill the box from the cli"},
{t:"Good to know",p:"Element ID Settings",u:"element-id-settings.html#good-to-know",b:"id=\"good-to-know\">Good to know # Open it from the flyout arrow on the Data panel title, or Shift+Click either element ID button; it is the same window."},
{t:"Ask AI",p:"AI (beta)",u:"ask-ai.html",b:"Beta. A chat panel that turns a plain-words request into a working pyNavis tool on the ribbon, and revises it when you ask."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Ask AI",u:"ask-ai.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # You type what the tool should do. The panel sends that, together with the pyNavis authoring guide, to the model you chose in AI Settings. The reply comes back as a short explanation plus the files of one bundle: bundle.yaml , script.py and sometimes config.py . Nothing touches disk until you click Create tool . Then the bundle is written into AI.extension under you"},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Ask AI",u:"ask-ai.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Click AI Settings once, pick a provider, paste your key, and click Test until the status line says it connected. Click Ask AI to show the panel and dock it beside the view. Describe the tool in plain words. If it depends on property names, select a few representative items first and tick Attach selection : the model then sees the real category and property names "},
{t:"What leaves your machine",p:"Ask AI",u:"ask-ai.html#what-leaves-your-machine",b:"id=\"what-leaves-your-machine\">What leaves your machine # Every request carries the authoring guide and your chat so far. Only when you ticked the box, it also carries the selection summary: the item count, the category names and the property names under each, capped at a few kilobytes. Never a value: a value is project data (a source file, a client, a project number in a custom tab), so the reader"},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Ask AI",u:"ask-ai.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Beta. Read the code before you run it. The model works from the guide and from what you say; it cannot see your model unless you attach the selection, and it cannot run the tool itself. One bundle per conversation, with only those three files. The model cannot write helper modules, hooks, dock panels or icons; generated tools get a lettered icon in one of five colour"},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Ask AI",u:"ask-ai.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Use it as a starting point: create the tool, then open the folder and edit script.py by hand like any other bundle. Reload is not even needed for a script edit. Prefer your own chatbot? Copy authoring guide in the panel's footer puts the same guide on the clipboard. Paste it, ask for the tool, and save the files it gives you into a *.pushbutton folder yourself. The aut"},
{t:"Good to know",p:"Ask AI",u:"ask-ai.html#good-to-know",b:"id=\"good-to-know\">Good to know # Anthropic requests send the guide as a cached prompt block, so from the second message on most of it is read from the provider's cache rather than billed in full. The Longest answer setting caps a reply; if the transcript says the answer hit the limit, raise it and ask again."},
{t:"AI Settings",p:"AI (beta)",u:"ai-settings.html",b:"The provider, endpoint, model and API key Ask AI uses: the Settings window opened on its AI section."},
{t:"What it does",p:"AI Settings",u:"ai-settings.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Opens the ordinary Settings window scrolled to AI assistant (beta) . Two providers: Anthropic , and OpenAI-compatible , which covers OpenAI itself, Azure OpenAI, Ollama, LM Studio and most local servers through one base URL. The model is a list you can also type into. Test sends one tiny request with the values as they stand and reports what came back."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"AI Settings",u:"ai-settings.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Pick the provider. For a local server choose OpenAI-compatible and set the base URL, for example http://localhost:11434/v1 ; leave the key empty. Paste the key. It is stored encrypted for your Windows account in %APPDATA%\\pyNavis\\secrets.json , never in config.json , so sharing your config or a screenshot of it never shares the key. Click Test , then Save . Ask A"},
{t:"Limitations",p:"AI Settings",u:"ai-settings.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # The key is readable only by the Windows account that saved it. A copied secrets.json reads as \"no key\" on another machine. Provider, base URL, model and longest answer live in config.json under ai , like every other setting."},
{t:"Open AI Folder",p:"AI (beta)",u:"open-ai-folder.html",b:"Explorer on the folder where Ask AI writes its tools."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Open AI Folder",u:"open-ai-folder.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Opens %APPDATA%\\pyNavis\\extensions\\AI.extension\\AI.tab\\Generated.panel in Explorer. Every generated tool is an ordinary bundle folder there: read it, edit it, copy it into your own extension, or delete it. Before the first tool exists it says so instead of opening an empty window."},
{t:"Good to know",p:"Open AI Folder",u:"open-ai-folder.html#good-to-know",b:"id=\"good-to-know\">Good to know # Deleting a tool's folder and clicking Reload removes it from the ribbon. Renaming the folder changes its bundle key, so a shortcut bound to it in the Shortcuts editor would need rebinding."},
{t:"Rename",p:"Viewpoints",u:"rename.html",b:"Batch rename saved viewpoints with find and replace, numbering, prefixes and case cleanup, with a live preview."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Rename",u:"rename.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # The tool for a viewpoint tree that arrived from three people with three naming conventions. Tick the views, choose an operation, watch the preview, apply."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Rename",u:"rename.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Click Rename and tick the viewpoints to change. Choose an operation: find and replace, plain or regular expression; a prefix and a suffix; sequential numbering with a start and a padding; or a case cleanup. Read the preview. Collisions, two views ending up with the same name, are flagged as you type, and nothing applies while one remains. Click Apply . The toast "},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Rename",u:"rename.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Folders are renamed like viewpoints, but their contents are not renumbered as a unit; tick the children for that."},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Rename",u:"rename.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Number a folder of views in walk order before exporting a report, so the pages come out in sequence. Strip a date suffix off fifty views in one go."},
{t:"Delete",p:"Viewpoints",u:"delete.html",b:"Bulk delete saved viewpoints, empty folders and animations, which the tree does one at a time."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Delete",u:"delete.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Search and tick, or pick out views by type, by being duplicates, or by having comments, then delete them as one operation."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Delete",u:"delete.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Click Delete . Search or use the pickers to tick what should go. Confirm. The dialog names how many items will be removed, because this changes the document. The toast reports what was removed and anything that failed."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Delete",u:"delete.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Deleting is one undo step in Navisworks, so Ctrl + Z works, but check the count in the confirmation first."},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Delete",u:"delete.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Clear out every view with a comment after the comments have been actioned."},
{t:"Manage",p:"Viewpoints",u:"manage.html",b:"Sort, move, create folders and purge empty ones in the saved viewpoint tree."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Manage",u:"manage.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Where Rename fixes names, this fixes structure. The dialog stays open and applies each action as you choose it, re-reading the tree afterwards so the next action is never planned against a stale picture."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Manage",u:"manage.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Click Manage . Sort the tree A to Z, move ticked items into a folder, create a folder, or purge folders that hold no viewpoints. Each applies at once."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Manage",u:"manage.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # A move that is not legal, such as a folder into itself, is refused with a reason rather than half applied. Purge asks for confirmation naming how many folders it would remove."},
{t:"Section Fit",p:"Viewpoints",u:"section-fit.html",b:"Fit the six section planes to the selection, turned to the objects rather than the world."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Section Fit",u:"section-fit.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Navisworks' own Fit Section to Selection gives a box square to the world, so a diagonal run of pipe gets four corners of empty space. This gives planes parallel to the run, with a little padding so faces do not graze the geometry."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Section Fit",u:"section-fit.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Select the objects. Click Section Fit . The toast reports the box it made."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Section Fit",u:"section-fit.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Inside the snap angle, 1.5 degrees by default, the box stays square to the world, because a small tilt costs every plane's alignment and buys nothing. A selection with no geometry, such as a folder node, has nothing to fit."},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Section Fit",u:"section-fit.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Section to one room's worth of services before a walkthrough, then nudge the faces with Section Nudge."},
{t:"Good to know",p:"Section Fit",u:"section-fit.html#good-to-know",b:"id=\"good-to-know\">Good to know # The padding starts at 150 mm. Both settings are shared with Section Plan in section_planes.json ."},
{t:"Section Plan",p:"Viewpoints",u:"section-plan.html",b:"Section Fit, then an orthographic plan view of the box, square on."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Section Plan",u:"section-plan.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Fits the planes the same way Section Fit does, then swings the camera to an orthographic plan of them. The camera turns with the box, so the objects sit square on screen rather than skewed across it."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Section Plan",u:"section-plan.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Select the objects and click Section Plan ."},
{t:"Good to know",p:"Section Plan",u:"section-plan.html#good-to-know",b:"id=\"good-to-know\">Good to know # Plan has no Shift+Click of its own; change the padding and snap angle from Section Fit."},
{t:"Section Clear",p:"Viewpoints",u:"section-clear.html",b:"Switch sectioning off without moving the camera."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Section Clear",u:"section-clear.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Turns the section planes off and leaves the view exactly where it is, so you keep the angle you worked to get and only lose the clipping."},
{t:"Copy State",p:"Viewpoints",u:"copy-state.html",b:"Copy the section planes, the hidden items or the appearance overrides to disk, to put back later."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Copy State",u:"copy-state.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # The answer to setting up a section, a set of hidden items or a colour scheme and then needing to look at something else. Pick a kind of state and it is written under %APPDATA%\\pyNavis\\viewstate\\ keyed to the document, so it is still there in another session. The Copy State switch: section, hidden items, appearance."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Copy State",u:"copy-state.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Click Copy State and pick section, hidden items or appearance. Change whatever you need to. Click Paste State to put it back."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Copy State",u:"copy-state.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # One copy of each kind per document. Copying section again replaces the earlier copy."},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Copy State",u:"copy-state.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Copy the appearance overrides of a colour-coded review, clear them to take clean screenshots, paste them back."},
{t:"Paste State",p:"Viewpoints",u:"paste-state.html",b:"Put back a kind of state copied earlier with Copy State."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Paste State",u:"paste-state.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Offers only the kinds actually copied for this document and restores the one you pick. With nothing copied yet it says so instead of showing an empty picker."},
{t:"Apply Speeds",p:"Viewpoints",u:"apply-speeds.html",b:"Push your walk speed, turn speed and field of view at the current view, in one keystroke."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Apply Speeds",u:"apply-speeds.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Navisworks resets navigation speed constantly: every recalled viewpoint brings its own, and a model in the wrong units makes walking either a crawl or a sprint. This puts your numbers back. The speeds window shared by both buttons: linear speed with a unit, angular speed, field of view, and a checkbox against each."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Apply Speeds",u:"apply-speeds.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Shift+Click once to set the three values. The defaults are 30 m/s, 45 degrees per second and 84 degrees. Untick any a reset should leave alone. Whenever walking feels wrong, press Alt + Q ."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Apply Speeds",u:"apply-speeds.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Field of view needs a perspective view. In an orthographic one it warns and applies the two speeds only."},
{t:"Good to know",p:"Apply Speeds",u:"apply-speeds.html#good-to-know",b:"id=\"good-to-know\">Good to know # The unit dropdown converts the number rather than relabelling it, so switching m/s to ft/s turns 30 into 98.4252. The speed is stored in the unit you typed and converted to the document's units when applied, so it means the same thing in a model drawn in feet."},
{t:"Speeds to Saved",p:"Viewpoints",u:"speeds-to-saved.html",b:"Write the same speeds and field of view into saved viewpoints you pick, without loading any of them."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Speeds to Saved",u:"speeds-to-saved.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # A set of views handed over by someone else navigates the way they liked. This rewrites the ticked views with your numbers, each one copied, its camera edited and swapped back, so nothing is loaded and the whole run is one undo step."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Speeds to Saved",u:"speeds-to-saved.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Press Alt + Z or click Speeds to Saved . The values about to be written are named at the top of the browser. Tick views or whole folders and click OK. The progress window can stop the run part way, keeping what was written."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Speeds to Saved",u:"speeds-to-saved.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Folders have no camera and animations keep theirs on their own cuts; both are counted and skipped. On some Navisworks releases a view has to be rebuilt to take the change, and the toast says plainly when redlines and appearance overrides were not carried over."},
{t:"Viewpoint Tracker",p:"Viewpoints",u:"viewpoint-tracker.html",b:"A dock panel that always says which saved view you are looking through, and whether you are still on it."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Viewpoint Tracker",u:"viewpoint-tracker.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Two lines, live. The top line reads \"Active viewpoint\" while the camera still sits where the saved view put it and \"Last viewpoint\" once you have walked off. The bottom line is the view's name, sized to fit the panel. Click the name to go back."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Viewpoint Tracker",u:"viewpoint-tracker.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Click Viewpoint Tracker on the ribbon to show the panel. Dock it wherever suits; the button follows the panel's visibility. Recall views as usual. The panel updates within a quarter of a second of the camera settling."},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Viewpoint Tracker",u:"viewpoint-tracker.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # Before any saved view has been current it shows \"No viewpoint selected\". View > Windows lists the panel by its slot name, \"pyNavis Panel N\", not by its title."},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Viewpoint Tracker",u:"viewpoint-tracker.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Presenting: the audience can see which view is on screen without you saying so. Tracker Window in the panel flyout is the same two lines as a floating always-on-top window, for when the dock panel is in the way."},
{t:"Section Nudge",p:"Viewpoints",u:"section-nudge.html",b:"A remote for the section box: faces named the way you see them, moves that follow the screen, a known step per press."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Section Nudge",u:"section-nudge.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # Dragging a section handle is imprecise and easy to overshoot. This panel moves the box or one of its faces a known distance per press, every press one undo step, from buttons or from the keyboard."},
{t:"Adjust: one face at a time",p:"Section Nudge",u:"section-nudge.html#adjust-one-face-at-a-time",b:"id=\"adjust-one-face-at-a-time\">Adjust: one face at a time # The six faces sit in an unfolded box and are named the way you see them: Front is the face looking at you, Left is on the screen's left. The names follow the camera, so orbit round the model and Front is still the face in front of you; the small axis label under each name tells you which world axis that currently is. Click a face, then In"},
{t:"Move: the whole box",p:"Section Nudge",u:"section-nudge.html#move-the-whole-box",b:"id=\"move-the-whole-box\">Move: the whole box # The Move tab slides all six planes together: left, right, up, down on the screen, nearer or farther from you. Two frames to choose from. World axes snaps each move to the world axis nearest the screen direction, like the compass ring round the ViewCube, so the box stays on the building's grid. Screen follows the camera exactly, like the cube itself, so"},
{t:"The step",p:"Section Nudge",u:"section-nudge.html#the-step",b:"id=\"the-step\">The step # Halve and double buttons, presets in the document's units, and a box to type a value. On an imperial document the box reads and writes feet and inches the way Revit does: 0.5 is half a foot and shows as 0' 6\", 0 0 1/8 is an eighth of an inch, and 1' 6\", 6 1/2\", 18\" and 1-6 all work."},
{t:"Keys and chords",p:"Section Nudge",u:"section-nudge.html#keys-and-chords",b:"id=\"keys-and-chords\">Keys and chords # With the panel focused: 0 to 6 pick a target, Tab cycles; Up and Down nudge a face; arrows move the box across the screen and Page Up and Page Down bring it nearer or push it farther; plus and minus change the step; Enter applies a typed step. Over the 3D view, no panel needed: Ctrl + Alt with Up and Down nudge, with Left and Right change the target, with Pag"},
{t:"Limitations",p:"Section Nudge",u:"section-nudge.html#limitations",b:"id=\"limitations\">Limitations # In Box mode, shrinking a face past the opposite face is refused. The readout follows changes made elsewhere once a second, so a handle dragged in the view shows up after a beat."},
{t:"Other uses",p:"Section Nudge",u:"section-nudge.html#other-uses",b:"id=\"other-uses\">Other uses # Fit to selection and Sectioning off sit at the bottom of the panel, so a whole sectioning job stays in one place. Step through a building floor by floor: set the step to the storey height and press Up."},
{t:"Tracker Window",p:"Viewpoints",u:"tracker-window.html",b:"The viewpoint tracker as a floating window that stays on top of everything."},
{t:"What it does",p:"Tracker Window",u:"tracker-window.html#what-it-does",b:"id=\"what-it-does\">What it does # The same two lines as the Viewpoint Tracker panel in a floating window that stays on top, for when the dock panel is in the way or you are walking the model with another application on screen. Both run the same code, so they can never disagree."},
{t:"How to use it",p:"Tracker Window",u:"tracker-window.html#how-to-use-it",b:"id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it # Open the Viewpoints panel flyout and click Tracker Window . Click the name to go back to that view. Clicking the button again focuses the window that is already open rather than opening a second."}
];
